1. What is man?

man stands for Manual.

It is used to read the documentation/help for Linux commands.

In simple language:

man = Linux's built-in instruction book.

For example, if you don't know how touch works:

man touch

Linux will show you the complete manual for the touch command.

2. Basic Syntax

man command

Example:

man ls

This opens the manual page for ls.

Other examples:

man cd
man mkdir
man cp
man mv
man rm
man touch

3. Lab Practice

Step 1: Open the manual for touch
man touch

You will see information similar to:

TOUCH(1)

a) NAME
       touch - change file timestamps

b) SYNOPSIS
       touch [OPTION]... FILE...

c) DESCRIPTION
       Update the access and modification times of each FILE...

Don't worry if it looks complicated at first.

=> The most important sections are:

NAME

Tells you what the command does.

SYNOPSIS

Shows the basic syntax of the command.

DESCRIPTION

Explains how the command works.

OPTIONS

Shows different options you can use.

4. How to exit man

When you run:

man touch

you enter the manual viewer.

To exit:

q

Just press q.

5. Searching inside man

Suppose you run:

man touch

and want to find the word reference.

Press:

/reference

then press Enter.

The manual will search for that word.

press 'n' for Next result

Press 'N' for Previous result

6. Moving inside the manual

When reading a long manual:

| Key     | Meaning                |
| ------- | ---------------------- |
| `↑`     | Move up                |
| `↓`     | Move down              |
| `Space` | Next page              |
| `b`     | Previous page          |
| `q`     | Quit                   |
| `/word` | Search for word        |
| `n`     | Next search result     |
| `N`     | Previous search result |

7. Example: man ls

Run:

man ls

Look for the SYNOPSIS section.

You will see something similar to:

ls [OPTION]... [FILE]...

This means ls can be used with options and files/directories.

For example:

ls -l

To understand what -l does, you can search inside the manual:

/-l

press Enter

8. man vs --help

You will commonly see both:

a) man ls

and:

b) ls --help

=> --help

Usually gives you a quick summary.

=> Usually gives you more detailed documentation.
